<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<section xml:id="section-dot-product" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>The Dot Product</title>

<objectives>
<introduction>
<p>
After completing this section, you should be able to:
</p>
</introduction>

<ul>
<li>
<p>
Understand the dot product as a way of combining two vectors into a single
number that measures the extent to which they point in the same direction.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Interpret the sign of the dot product geometrically&#8212;positive for an
acute angle, negative for an obtuse angle, and zero for perpendicular
vectors&#8212;and recognize orthogonality as the vanishing of the dot product.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Understand how the dot product encodes the angle between two vectors,
connecting the algebra of vectors to the Law of Cosines.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Interpret the projection of one vector onto another as the component of one
vector in the direction of the other, and understand the decomposition of a
vector into parts parallel and perpendicular to a given direction.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Understand work as a physical measure of the effect of a force along a
direction of motion, and appreciate why the dot product is the natural tool
for expressing it.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Explain how the dot product is used in data science to measure similarity:
represent preferences as vectors, compute the cosine similarity and the
angular distance between them, interpret the result, and extend the
computation from two dimensions to <m>\mathbb R^n</m>.
</p>
</li>
</ul>
</objectives>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-dot-product">
<title>The Dot Product and the Angle between Two Vectors</title>

<p>
Consider two vectors <m>\mathbf u</m> and <m>\mathbf v</m>, and let
<m>\mathbf w = \mathbf u - \mathbf v</m>, as shown in
<xref ref="fig-law-of-cosines"/>. By the law of cosines,
<md xml:id="eq-1" number="yes">
\|\mathbf w\|^2 = \|\mathbf u\|^2 + \|\mathbf v\|^2 - 2\|\mathbf u\|\|\mathbf v\|\cos\theta.
</md>
</p>
<figure xml:id="fig-law-of-cosines">
<caption>
The vectors <m>\mathbf u</m>, <m>\mathbf v</m>, and
<m>\mathbf w = \mathbf u - \mathbf v</m>, together with the angle
<m>\theta</m> between <m>\mathbf u</m> and <m>\mathbf v</m>.
</caption>
<image width="55%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-law-of-cosines">
<diagram dimensions="(300,300)" margins="10">
<definition>u=(1.5,4)</definition>
<definition>v=(4,1)</definition>
<definition>w=u-v</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-0.5,-0.5,4.5,4.5)">
<vector at="vec-u" v="u" tail="(0,0)" stroke="blue"/>
<vector at="vec-v" v="v" tail="(0,0)" stroke="blue"/>
<vector at="vec-w" v="w" tail="v" stroke="magenta"/>
<label at="u-label" anchor="(0.75,2)" alignment="nw" color="blue">
<m>\mathbf u</m>
</label>
<label at="v-label" anchor="(2,0.5)" alignment="se" color="blue">
<m>\mathbf v</m>
</label>
<label at="w-label" anchor="(2.75,2.5)" alignment="ne" color="magenta">
<m>\mathbf w</m>
</label>
<angle-marker at="angle-theta" points="(v,(0,0),u)" radius="18" clear-background="no">
<m>\theta</m>
</angle-marker>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="A triangle formed by the vectors u, v, and w, where w equals u minus v. The vectors u and v are drawn tail to tail from the origin, and w connects the tip of v to the tip of u. The angle theta lies between u and v at the origin.">
  <annotation ref="vec-u" text="Vector u, drawn from the origin."/>
  <annotation ref="vec-v" text="Vector v, drawn from the origin."/>
  <annotation ref="vec-w" text="Vector w, equal to u minus v, drawn from the tip of v to the tip of u."/>
  <annotation ref="angle-theta" text="The angle theta between u and v, located at the origin."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>


<proof>
  <p>
    Watch the video below for a proof of the law of cosines using vectors.
  </p>
  <figure xml:id="fig-law-of-cosines-video">
  <caption>Proving the law of cosines: drop a perpendicular from the tip of
  <m>\mathbf u</m> and apply the Pythagorean theorem to the right triangle
  whose hypotenuse is <m>\mathbf w = \mathbf u - \mathbf v</m>.</caption>
  <video xml:id="video-law-of-cosines"
         source="videos/law_of_cosines_1080p.mp4"
         preview="videos/previews/law_of_cosines_1080p.jpg"
         width="90%" aspect="16:9"/>
</figure>
</proof>
<p>
Writing <m>\mathbf u = \langle u_1, u_2, u_3\rangle</m> and
<m>\mathbf v = \langle v_1, v_2, v_3\rangle</m>, note that
<m>\mathbf w = \mathbf u - \mathbf v</m>, and hence
<md xml:id="eq-2" number="yes">
\begin{aligned}
\|\mathbf w\|^2 \amp = \|\mathbf u - \mathbf v\|^2 \\
\amp = (u_1-v_1)^2 + (u_2-v_2)^2 + (u_3-v_3)^2 \\
\amp = (u_1^2+u_2^2+u_3^2) + (v_1^2+v_2^2+v_3^2) \\
\amp \qquad - 2(u_1v_1+u_2v_2+u_3v_3).
\end{aligned}
</md>
</p>
<p>
Also, from the law of cosines in <xref ref="eq-1"/> we have
<md xml:id="eq-3" number="yes">
\|\mathbf w\|^2 = (u_1^2+u_2^2+u_3^2) + (v_1^2+v_2^2+v_3^2) - 2\|\mathbf u\|\|\mathbf v\|\cos\theta.
</md>
</p>
<p>
From <xref ref="eq-2"/> and <xref ref="eq-3"/>, it follows that
<m>u_1v_1+u_2v_2+u_3v_3 = \|\mathbf u\|\|\mathbf v\|\cos\theta</m>, and therefore
the angle between the two vectors <m>\mathbf u</m> and <m>\mathbf v</m>
can be evaluated using the following equation.
</p>

<theorem xml:id="thm-angle-formula">
<title>Angle between Two Vectors</title>
<statement>
<p>
Let <m>\mathbf u = \langle u_1, u_2, u_3\rangle</m> and
<m>\mathbf v = \langle v_1, v_2, v_3\rangle</m> be two nonzero vectors, and let
<m>\theta</m>, with <m>0 \le \theta \le \pi</m>, be the angle between them.
Then
<md xml:id="eq-4" number="yes">
\begin{aligned}
\cos\theta \amp = \frac{u_1v_1+u_2v_2+u_3v_3}{\|\mathbf u\|\|\mathbf v\|} \\
\iff\quad \theta \amp = \cos^{-1}\left(\frac{u_1v_1+u_2v_2+u_3v_3}{\|\mathbf u\|\|\mathbf v\|}\right).
\end{aligned}
</md>
</p>
</statement>
</theorem>

<definition xml:id="def-dot-product">
<title>Dot Product</title>
<statement>
<p>
The term <m>u_1v_1+u_2v_2+u_3v_3</m> in the numerator of <xref ref="eq-4"/>
is known as the <term>dot product</term> of the two vectors
<m>\mathbf u = \langle u_1,u_2,u_3\rangle</m> and
<m>\mathbf v = \langle v_1,v_2,v_3\rangle</m>, and is denoted
<m>\mathbf u \cdot \mathbf v</m>.
</p>
<p>
<md xml:id="eq-5" number="yes">
\mathbf u \cdot \mathbf v = u_1v_1+u_2v_2+u_3v_3.
</md>
</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<example xml:id="example-dot-product-compute">
<title>Computing a dot product</title>
<statement>
<p>
Let <m>\mathbf u = \langle 1,2,3\rangle</m> and
<m>\mathbf v = \langle -2,0,4\rangle</m>. Compute
<m>\mathbf u \cdot \mathbf v</m>, and use it to find the angle between
<m>\mathbf u</m> and <m>\mathbf v</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Multiply the corresponding components and add:
<md>
\mathbf u \cdot \mathbf v = (1)(-2) + (2)(0) + (3)(4) = -2 + 0 + 12 = 10.
</md>
</p>
<p>
Since the dot product is positive, the angle between the two vectors is acute.
To find it, use <xref ref="eq-4"/>:
<md>
\begin{aligned}
\|\mathbf u\| \amp = \sqrt{1+4+9} = \sqrt{14} \\
\|\mathbf v\| \amp = \sqrt{4+0+16} = \sqrt{20} \\
\cos\theta \amp = \frac{\mathbf u \cdot \mathbf v}{\|\mathbf u\|\|\mathbf v\|}
= \frac{10}{\sqrt{14}\,\sqrt{20}} \approx 0.5976 \\
\theta \amp = \cos^{-1}(0.5976) \approx 0.930 \text{ radians} \approx 53.3^{\circ}.
\end{aligned}
</md>
</p>
</solution>
</example>

<remark xml:id="remark-orthogonal">
<title>Orthogonal vectors</title>
<p>
If the two vectors <m>\mathbf u</m> and <m>\mathbf v</m> are orthogonal, then
<m>\theta = \pi/2</m>, which means <m>\mathbf u \cdot \mathbf v = 0</m>.
Conversely, if <m>\mathbf u</m> and <m>\mathbf v</m> are two vectors such that
<m>\mathbf u \cdot \mathbf v = 0</m>, then <m>\mathbf u</m> and <m>\mathbf v</m>
are orthogonal.
</p>
<p>
<md xml:id="eq-6" number="yes">
\mathbf u \cdot \mathbf v = 0 \iff \mathbf u \perp \mathbf v.
</md>
</p>
</remark>

<example xml:id="example-right-triangle">
<title>A right triangle detected with the dot product</title>
<statement>
<p>
Consider the triangle with vertices <m>A(1,-1)</m>, <m>B(1,1)</m>, and
<m>C(2,0)</m>. Show that this is a right triangle using the dot product
and the orthogonality criterion <xref ref="eq-6"/>.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
<md>
\begin{aligned}
\overrightarrow{CA} \amp = \langle 1-2,\,-1-0\rangle = \langle -1,-1\rangle \\
\overrightarrow{CB} \amp = \langle 1-2,\,1-0\rangle = \langle -1,1\rangle \\
\overrightarrow{CA}\cdot\overrightarrow{CB} \amp = 1 - 1 = 0 \implies \overrightarrow{CA} \perp \overrightarrow{CB}.
\end{aligned}
</md>
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-right-triangle">
<caption>
The triangle with vertices <m>A(1,-1)</m>, <m>B(1,1)</m>, <m>C(2,0)</m>,
together with the vectors <m>\overrightarrow{CA}</m> and <m>\overrightarrow{CB}</m> meeting at a
right angle at <m>C</m>.
</caption>
<image width="50%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-right-triangle">
<diagram dimensions="(320,320)" margins="10">
<definition>A=(1,-1)</definition>
<definition>B=(1,1)</definition>
<definition>C=(2,0)</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-2.5,-2.5,2.5,2.5)">
<grid-axes at="grid" xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>
<triangle at="triangle" vertices="(A,B,C)" labels="A,B,C"
          show-vertices="yes" point-fill="black" stroke="black"/>
<vector at="vec-ca" v="A-C" tail="C" stroke="blue" thickness="4" alignment="sw">
<m>\overrightarrow{CA}</m>
</vector>
<vector at="vec-cb" v="B-C" tail="C" stroke="red" thickness="4" alignment="ne">
<m>\overrightarrow{CB}</m>
</vector>
<angle-marker at="right-angle" points="(B,C,A)" clear-background="yes"/>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="A coordinate grid showing triangle A B C, with A at 1 comma negative 1, B at 1 comma 1, and C at 2 comma 0, together with the vectors C A and C B, which meet at a right angle at C.">
  <annotation ref="grid" text="A coordinate grid with x and y axes."/>
  <annotation ref="triangle" text="Triangle A B C, plotted on the grid."/>
  <annotation ref="vec-ca" text="The vector C A, drawn in blue from C to A."/>
  <annotation ref="vec-cb" text="The vector C B, drawn in red from C to B."/>
  <annotation ref="right-angle" text="A right angle marker at C, confirming that C A and C B are perpendicular."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>
</solution>
</example>

<example xml:id="example-remaining-angles">
<title>The remaining angles of the triangle</title>
<statement>
<p>
For the triangle of <xref ref="example-right-triangle"/>
(shown again in <xref ref="fig-right-triangle"/>), calculate the remaining angles.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
For the angle between the sides <m>\overrightarrow{AC}</m> and <m>\overrightarrow{AB}</m>, we have
<md>
\begin{aligned}
\overrightarrow{AC} \amp = \langle 2-1,\,0+1\rangle = \langle 1,1\rangle \implies \|\overrightarrow{AC}\| = \sqrt2 \\
\overrightarrow{AB} \amp = \langle 1-1,\,1+1\rangle = \langle 0,2\rangle \implies \|\overrightarrow{AB}\| = 2 \\
\overrightarrow{AB}\cdot\overrightarrow{AC} \amp = 0 + 2 = 2 \\
\cos\theta \amp = \frac{\overrightarrow{AB}\cdot\overrightarrow{AC}}{\|\overrightarrow{AB}\|\|\overrightarrow{AC}\|}
= \frac{2}{2\sqrt2} = \frac{1}{\sqrt2} \implies \theta = \frac{\pi}{4}.
\end{aligned}
</md>
</p>
</solution>
</example>

<paragraphs xml:id="par-properties">
<title>Properties of the Dot Product</title>

<fact xml:id="fact-properties">
<statement>
<p>
If <m>\mathbf u</m>, <m>\mathbf v</m>, and <m>\mathbf w</m> are any vectors
and <m>c</m> is a scalar, then
</p>
<p>
<ol>
<li><p><m>\mathbf u \cdot \mathbf v = \mathbf v \cdot \mathbf u</m></p></li>
<li><p><m>(c\mathbf u)\cdot \mathbf v = \mathbf u \cdot (c\mathbf v) = c(\mathbf u \cdot \mathbf v)</m></p></li>
<li><p><m>\mathbf u \cdot (\mathbf v + \mathbf w) = \mathbf u \cdot \mathbf v + \mathbf u \cdot \mathbf w</m></p></li>
<li><p><m>\mathbf u \cdot \mathbf u = \|\mathbf u\|^2</m></p></li>
<li><p><m>\mathbf 0 \cdot \mathbf u = 0</m></p></li>
</ol>
</p>
</statement>

<proof>
<p>
Write <m>\mathbf u = \langle u_1,u_2,u_3\rangle</m>,
<m>\mathbf v = \langle v_1,v_2,v_3\rangle</m>, and
<m>\mathbf w = \langle w_1,w_2,w_3\rangle</m>. Each property follows from
<xref ref="eq-5"/> together with the corresponding property of real numbers.
</p>
<ol>
<li>
<p>
Multiplication of real numbers is commutative, so
<md>
\mathbf u \cdot \mathbf v = u_1v_1+u_2v_2+u_3v_3
= v_1u_1+v_2u_2+v_3u_3 = \mathbf v \cdot \mathbf u.
</md>
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Since <m>c\mathbf u = \langle cu_1,cu_2,cu_3\rangle</m>,
<md>
\begin{aligned}
(c\mathbf u)\cdot \mathbf v \amp = cu_1v_1+cu_2v_2+cu_3v_3 \\
\amp = c(u_1v_1+u_2v_2+u_3v_3) = c(\mathbf u \cdot \mathbf v),
\end{aligned}
</md>
and the same computation applied to <m>c\mathbf v = \langle cv_1,cv_2,cv_3\rangle</m>
gives <m>\mathbf u \cdot (c\mathbf v) = c(\mathbf u \cdot \mathbf v)</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Adding componentwise and then distributing,
<md>
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf u \cdot (\mathbf v + \mathbf w)
\amp = u_1(v_1+w_1) + u_2(v_2+w_2) + u_3(v_3+w_3) \\
\amp = (u_1v_1+u_2v_2+u_3v_3) + (u_1w_1+u_2w_2+u_3w_3) \\
\amp = \mathbf u \cdot \mathbf v + \mathbf u \cdot \mathbf w.
\end{aligned}
</md>
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Taking <m>\mathbf v = \mathbf u</m> in <xref ref="eq-5"/>, and recalling that
<m>\|\mathbf u\| = \sqrt{u_1^2+u_2^2+u_3^2}</m>,
<md>
\mathbf u \cdot \mathbf u = u_1^2+u_2^2+u_3^2
= \left(\sqrt{u_1^2+u_2^2+u_3^2}\right)^2 = \|\mathbf u\|^2.
</md>
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Every component of <m>\mathbf 0</m> is zero, so
<m>\mathbf 0 \cdot \mathbf u = 0u_1+0u_2+0u_3 = 0</m>.
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</proof>
</fact>
</paragraphs>

</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-projection">
<title>Projection of <m>\mathbf u</m> in the Direction of <m>\mathbf v</m></title>

<p>
The dot product measures how much two vectors point in the same direction. This
section turns that measurement into a vector: given <m>\mathbf u</m> and
<m>\mathbf v</m>, we ask how much of <m>\mathbf u</m> lies along <m>\mathbf v</m>.
The answer is the <term>projection</term> of <m>\mathbf u</m> onto
<m>\mathbf v</m>, and it lets us split any vector into a part that points along
<m>\mathbf v</m> and a part perpendicular to it. That decomposition is what makes
the dot product useful in practice&#8212;it is how we find the component of a
force along a direction of motion, as in
<xref ref="sec-work"/>, or the component of gravity down a slope.
</p>

<p>
The projection of <m>\mathbf u</m> in the direction of <m>\mathbf v</m> is
denoted <m>\text{proj}_{\mathbf v}\mathbf u</m>, and is shown in
<xref ref="fig-projection"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-projection">
<caption>
The projection <m>\text{proj}_{\mathbf v}\mathbf u</m> of <m>\mathbf u</m>
in the direction of <m>\mathbf v</m>, together with the perpendicular
component <m>\mathbf u - \text{proj}_{\mathbf v}\mathbf u</m>.
</caption>
<image width="55%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-projection">
<diagram dimensions="(340,260)" margins="10">
<coordinates bbox="(-0.5,-1,7,6)">
<vector at="vec-v" v="(6,0)" tail="(0,0)" stroke="blue"/>
<vector at="vec-u" v="(4,5)" tail="(0,0)" stroke="blue"/>
<vector at="vec-proj" v="(4,0)" tail="(0,0)" stroke="maroon" thickness="5"/>
<vector at="vec-perp" v="(0,5)" tail="(4,0)" stroke="maroon" thickness="5"/>
<label at="v-label" anchor="(6,-0.3)" alignment="s" color="blue">
<m>\mathbf v</m>
</label>
<label at="u-label" anchor="(2,2.5)" alignment="nw" color="blue">
<m>\mathbf u</m>
</label>
<label at="proj-label" anchor="(2,-0.3)" alignment="s" color="maroon">
<m>\mathbf u_{\parallel} = \text{proj}_{\mathbf v}\mathbf u</m>
</label>
<label at="perp-label" anchor="(4,2.5)" alignment="e" color="maroon">
<m>\mathbf u_{\perp} = \mathbf u - \mathbf u_{\parallel}</m>
</label>
<angle-marker at="angle-theta" points="((6,0),(0,0),(4,5))" radius="23" clear-background="no">
<m>\theta</m>
</angle-marker>
<angle-marker at="right-angle" points="((4,5),(4,0),(0,0))" clear-background="yes"/>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="A right triangle showing vector u as the hypotenuse, its projection onto v along the horizontal base, and the perpendicular segment u minus the projection of u onto v.">
  <annotation ref="vec-v" text="Vector v, along the horizontal direction."/>
  <annotation ref="vec-u" text="Vector u, drawn from the origin at angle theta above v."/>
  <annotation ref="vec-proj" text="The projection of u in the direction of v, lying along v."/>
  <annotation ref="vec-perp" text="The component of u perpendicular to v, equal to u minus the projection of u onto v."/>
  <annotation ref="angle-theta" text="The angle theta between u and v."/>
  <annotation ref="right-angle" text="A right angle where the perpendicular component meets v."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
The length of <m>\text{proj}_{\mathbf v}\mathbf u</m> is
<m>\left\|\text{proj}_{\mathbf v}\mathbf u\right\| = \|\mathbf u\|\cos\theta</m>,
and since its direction is the same as <m>\mathbf v</m>, we have
<md number="yes">
\text{proj}_{\mathbf v}\mathbf u
= (\|\mathbf u\|\cos\theta)\,\frac{\mathbf v}{\|\mathbf v\|}
= \left(\|\mathbf u\|\,\frac{\mathbf u\cdot\mathbf v}{\|\mathbf u\|\|\mathbf v\|}\right)\frac{\mathbf v}{\|\mathbf v\|}
= \left(\frac{\mathbf u\cdot\mathbf v}{\|\mathbf v\|^2}\right)\mathbf v.
</md>
</p>
<fact xml:id="fact-projection-formula">
<title>Projection Formula</title>
<statement>
<p>
<md xml:id="eq-proj" number="yes">
\text{proj}_{\mathbf v}\mathbf u = \left(\frac{\mathbf u\cdot\mathbf v}{\|\mathbf v\|^2}\right)\mathbf v.
</md>
</p>
</statement>
</fact>

<paragraphs xml:id="par-decomposition">
<title>Writing <m>\mathbf u</m> as Two Vectors, One Parallel and One Perpendicular to <m>\mathbf v</m></title>

<p>
As can be seen in <xref ref="fig-projection"/>,
<m>\text{proj}_{\mathbf v}\mathbf u</m> is parallel to <m>\mathbf v</m>, and
the vector <m>\mathbf u - \text{proj}_{\mathbf v}\mathbf u</m> is perpendicular
to it. Also, the sum of these two vectors equals <m>\mathbf u</m>. This means
we can write <m>\mathbf u</m> as
<md xml:id="eq-decomposition" number="yes">
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf u \amp = \text{proj}_{\mathbf v}\mathbf u + \left(\mathbf u - \text{proj}_{\mathbf v}\mathbf u\right) \\
\amp = \underbrace{\left(\frac{\mathbf u\cdot\mathbf v}{\|\mathbf v\|^2}\right)\mathbf v}_{\text{parallel to } \mathbf v}
\;+\; \underbrace{\left(\mathbf u - \left(\frac{\mathbf u\cdot\mathbf v}{\|\mathbf v\|^2}\right)\mathbf v\right)}_{\text{orthogonal to } \mathbf v}.
\end{aligned}
</md>
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-projection-shadow">
  <caption>The projection of <m>\overrightarrow{u}</m> onto <m>\overrightarrow{v}</m> as a shadow cast by light from above.</caption>
  <video xml:id="video-projection-shadow"
         source="videos/projection_formula_1080p.mp4"
         preview="videos/previews/projection_formula_1080p.jpg"
         width="90%" aspect="16:9"/>
</figure>

<example xml:id="example-decomposition">
<title>Decomposing a vector into parallel and perpendicular parts</title>
<statement>
<p>
Consider the two vectors <m>\mathbf v = \hat{\imath} + \hat{\jmath}</m> and
<m>\mathbf u = 2\hat{\imath} + 2\hat{\jmath} + 2\hat{k}</m>. Write <m>\mathbf u</m>
as a sum of two vectors, one of which is parallel to <m>\mathbf v</m> and the
other perpendicular to it.
</p>
</statement>

<solution>
<p>
Since the projection of <m>\mathbf u</m> in the direction of <m>\mathbf v</m>
is parallel to <m>\mathbf v</m>, we have
<md>
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf u \cdot \mathbf v \amp = (2\times1)+(2\times1)+(2\times0) = 4 \\
\|\mathbf v\| \amp = \sqrt{1+1} = \sqrt2 \\
\mathbf u_{\parallel} = \text{proj}_{\mathbf v}\mathbf u
\amp = \left(\frac{\mathbf u\cdot\mathbf v}{\|\mathbf v\|^2}\right)\mathbf v
= \frac{4}{2}(\hat{\imath}+\hat{\jmath}) = 2\hat{\imath} + 2\hat{\jmath}.
\end{aligned}
</md>
</p>
<p>
For the perpendicular component of <m>\mathbf u</m> to <m>\mathbf v</m>, we have
<md number="yes">
\mathbf u_{\perp} = \mathbf u - \text{proj}_{\mathbf v}\mathbf u
= (2\hat{\imath}+2\hat{\jmath}+2\hat{k}) - (2\hat{\imath}+2\hat{\jmath}) = 2\hat{k}.
</md>
</p>
<p>
To achieve a visual understanding of this decomposition, watch the video below.
</p>
<figure xml:id="fig-decompose-3d">
  <caption>Decomposing <m>\mathbf u = 2\hat{\imath} + 2\hat{\jmath} + 2\hat{k}</m> into
  <m>\mathbf u_{\parallel} = 2\hat{\imath} + 2\hat{\jmath}</m>, lying in the
  <m>xy</m>-plane along <m>\mathbf v</m>, and <m>\mathbf u_{\perp} = 2\hat{k}</m>,
  pointing straight up.</caption>
  <video xml:id="video-decompose-3d"
         source="videos/decompose_3d_v3_1080p.mp4"
         preview="videos/previews/decompose_3d_v3_1080p.jpg"
         width="90%" aspect="16:9"/>
</figure>
</solution>
</example>

<exercise xml:id="check-projection-decomposition">
<title>Concept Check</title>
<statement>
<p>
Everything in <xref ref="eq-decomposition"/> rests on the claim read off from
<xref ref="fig-projection"/>: that <m>\text{proj}_{\mathbf v}\mathbf u</m> is
parallel to <m>\mathbf v</m>, and that
<m>\mathbf u - \text{proj}_{\mathbf v}\mathbf u</m> is perpendicular to
<m>\mathbf v</m>. A picture is not a proof. Explain why each half of the claim
is true.
</p>
<p>
<ol marker="(a)">
<li>
<p>
For the parallel half, look at the formula for
<m>\text{proj}_{\mathbf v}\mathbf u</m> in
<xref ref="fact-projection-formula"/> and say what kind of expression it is.
Why does that observation alone settle the question?
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
For the perpendicular half, compute
<m>\left(\mathbf u - \text{proj}_{\mathbf v}\mathbf u\right)\cdot\mathbf v</m>.
Justify each step by naming the property of the dot product from
<xref ref="fact-properties"/> that you used, and then say what the value you
obtain tells you about the two vectors.
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</statement>
<hint>
<p>
In (b), write <m>\text{proj}_{\mathbf v}\mathbf u = c\,\mathbf v</m> where
<m>c = \dfrac{\mathbf u\cdot\mathbf v}{\|\mathbf v\|^2}</m> is a scalar. In that
form properties 2 and 3 of <xref ref="fact-properties"/> apply directly, and
property 4 will simplify <m>\mathbf v\cdot\mathbf v</m>.
</p>
</hint>
<solution>
<p>
(a) By <xref ref="fact-projection-formula"/>,
<md>
\text{proj}_{\mathbf v}\mathbf u = c\,\mathbf v,
\qquad c = \frac{\mathbf u\cdot\mathbf v}{\|\mathbf v\|^2},
</md>
so the projection is simply a number times <m>\mathbf v</m>. A scalar multiple
of <m>\mathbf v</m> points along <m>\mathbf v</m> when <m>c &gt; 0</m> and
opposite to <m>\mathbf v</m> when <m>c &lt; 0</m>; either way it is parallel to
<m>\mathbf v</m>. Nothing further needs to be checked.
</p>
<p>
(b) With <m>\text{proj}_{\mathbf v}\mathbf u = c\,\mathbf v</m> as above,
<md>
\begin{aligned}
\left(\mathbf u - c\,\mathbf v\right)\cdot\mathbf v
\amp = \mathbf u\cdot\mathbf v - (c\,\mathbf v)\cdot\mathbf v \\
\amp = \mathbf u\cdot\mathbf v - c\,(\mathbf v\cdot\mathbf v) \\
\amp = \mathbf u\cdot\mathbf v - c\,\|\mathbf v\|^2 \\
\amp = \mathbf u\cdot\mathbf v
- \frac{\mathbf u\cdot\mathbf v}{\|\mathbf v\|^2}\,\|\mathbf v\|^2 = 0.
\end{aligned}
</md>
</p>
<p>
The first line distributes the dot product over the difference, which is
properties 1 and 3 of <xref ref="fact-properties"/>; the second pulls the scalar
<m>c</m> out, which is property 2; the third replaces
<m>\mathbf v\cdot\mathbf v</m> by <m>\|\mathbf v\|^2</m>, which is property 4.
The last line just substitutes the value of <m>c</m>.
</p>
<p>
So the dot product is zero, and by <xref ref="remark-orthogonal"/> that is
exactly what it means for <m>\mathbf u - \text{proj}_{\mathbf v}\mathbf u</m> to
be perpendicular to <m>\mathbf v</m>.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

</paragraphs>

<paragraphs xml:id="par-brain-map-dot-product">
<title>The Big Picture</title>
<p>
The map below gathers the ideas of this section so far on one page:
the dot product and the angle between two vectors, and the projection of one vector onto another. An arrow between two topics means the second builds on the
first, and the gold arrows mark a derivation, where a formula follows from
the topic behind it. Every topic on the map is a link to the part of the section
where it is covered. For comfortable reading,
<url href="external/brain-map-dot-product.html" visual="">open the map full size in a new tab</url>.
</p>
</paragraphs>

<interactive xml:id="interactive-brain-map-dot-product" iframe="brain-map-dot-product.html" width="100%" aspect="16:9" preview="brain-map-dot-product-print.png">
<instructions>
<p>
Each box on the map is an idea from this section, and each arrow connects
an idea to the ones that build on it; the gold arrows mark a derivation,
where a formula follows from the idea behind it. Hover over a box to light
up its connections, and click it to jump to the part of the section where
it is covered.
</p>
</instructions>
</interactive>

</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-work">
<title>Application in Physics: Work</title>

<p>
Consider the scenario in which a constant force <m>\mathbf F</m> causes an object to
move from point <m>P</m> to point <m>Q</m>, as shown in
<xref ref="fig-work"/>. The vector <m>\overrightarrow{PQ}</m> is often denoted
<m>\mathbf D</m> and is referred to as the <term>displacement vector</term>.
The work done by the force is then
</p>

<definition xml:id="def-work">
<title>Work</title>
<statement>
<p>
<md xml:id="eq-work" number="yes">
\text{Work} = \mathbf F \cdot \mathbf D = \|\mathbf D\|\|\mathbf F\|\cos\theta,
</md>
where <m>\left\|\text{proj}_{\mathbf D}\mathbf F\right\| = \|\mathbf F\|\cos\theta</m>
is the magnitude of the force in the direction of motion, as computed in
<xref ref="sec-projection"/>.
</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<figure xml:id="fig-work-force">
  <caption>A constant force <m>\mathbf F</m> acting on an object causing a displacement from <m>A</m> to <m>B</m>.</caption>
  <video xml:id="video-work-force"
         source="videos/work_force_1080p.mp4"
         preview="videos/previews/work_force_1080p.jpg"
         width="90%" aspect="16:9"/>
</figure>

<figure xml:id="fig-work">
<caption>
A force <m>\mathbf F</m> displaces an object from <m>P</m> to <m>Q</m> along
the displacement vector <m>\mathbf D</m>. The reference segment shows the
magnitude of the force in the direction of motion, <m>\|\mathbf F\|\cos\theta</m>.
</caption>
<image width="60%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-work">
<diagram dimensions="(360,220)" margins="10">
<definition>P=(0,0)</definition>
<definition>Q=(4,0)</definition>
<definition>Fv=(2,1.5)</definition>
<definition>R=(6,0)</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-0.5,-1,7,3)">
<point at="point-p" p="P" alignment="sw"><m>P</m></point>
<point at="point-q" p="Q" alignment="s"><m>Q</m></point>
<vector at="vec-d" v="Q-P" tail="P" stroke="maroon" thickness="5" alignment="s" label-location="0.5">
<m>\mathbf D</m>
</vector>
<vector at="vec-f" v="Fv" tail="Q" stroke="blue" thickness="4" alignment="ne">
<m>\mathbf F</m>
</vector>
<line at="ref-line" endpoints="(Q,R)" stroke="lightgray" thickness="1"/>
<angle-marker at="angle-theta" points="(R,Q,(6,1.5))" radius="23" clear-background="no">
<m>\theta</m>
</angle-marker>
<label at="label-cos" anchor="(5,-0.4)" clear-background="no">
<m>\|\mathbf F\|\cos\theta</m>
</label>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="A force F acting at Q at angle theta above the direction of motion, together with the displacement vector D from P to Q, and a reference segment showing the magnitude of F times cosine theta, the component of F in the direction of motion." sonify="yes" >
  <annotation ref="point-p" text="The starting point P." sonify="yes"/>
  <annotation ref="point-q" text="The ending point Q." sonify="yes"/>
  <annotation ref="vec-d" text="The displacement vector D, from P to Q." sonify="yes"/>
  <annotation ref="vec-f" text="The force vector F, acting at Q at angle theta above the direction of motion." sonify="yes"/>
  <annotation ref="ref-line" text="A horizontal reference segment used to measure the angle theta." sonify="yes"/>
  <annotation ref="angle-theta" text="The angle theta between the force F and the direction of motion." sonify="yes"/>
  <annotation ref="label-cos" text="The magnitude of F in the direction of motion, F times cosine theta." sonify="yes"/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>

<example xml:id="example-work">
<title>Computing work done by a force</title>
<statement>
<p>
A force is given by the vector <m>\mathbf F = 8\hat{\imath} - 6\hat{\jmath} + 9\hat{k}</m>
and moves a particle from the point <m>A(0,10,8)</m> to the point
<m>B(6,12,20)</m>. Find the work done.
</p>
</statement>
<hint>
  <p>
    Consider using <xref ref="eq-work"/>, evaluating <m>\mathbf F \cdot \mathbf D</m>
    with the component formula <xref ref="eq-5"/>. For more help, watch the video below. </p>
    <figure xml:id="fig-work-hint">
  <caption>Set up the work computation: build the displacement vector first, then take the dot product. What do you get?</caption>
  <video xml:id="video-work-hint"
         source="videos/work_hint_v2_1080p.mp4"
         preview="videos/previews/work_hint_v2_1080p.jpg"
         width="90%" aspect="16:9"/>
</figure>
  </hint>
<solution>
<p>
The displacement vector is <m>\mathbf D = \overrightarrow{AB} = \langle 6,2,12\rangle</m>,
so by <xref ref="eq-work"/>, evaluated with <xref ref="eq-5"/>, the work done is
<md>
\text{Work} = \mathbf F \cdot \mathbf D
= \langle 8,-6,9\rangle \cdot \langle 6,2,12\rangle
= 48 - 12 + 108 = 144.
</md>
</p>
</solution>
</example>



<example xml:id="ex-inclined-plane">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Suppose a mass of <m>10\text{ kg}</m> is resting on an inclined plane. 
          Gravity exerts a force equivalent to <m>mg</m> on the object, where <m>g = 9.8\text{ m/s}^2</m> is 
          the gravitational acceleration. Suppose the incline is tilted at a <m>30^{\circ}</m> angle. 
          Compute the components of the force that are parallel and perpendicular to the inclined plane.
        </p>
      </statement>
      
      <solution>

<figure xml:id="fig-inclined-video">
  <caption>Decomposing gravity on a <m>30^{\circ}</m> incline: the parallel component
  <m>\overrightarrow{F}_{\parallel} = \operatorname{proj}_{\hat{v}}\overrightarrow{F}</m> pulls the mass down
  the slope, and the perpendicular component
  <m>\overrightarrow{F}_{\perp} = \overrightarrow{F} - \overrightarrow{F}_{\parallel}</m> presses it into the surface.</caption>
  <video xml:id="video-inclined-plane"
         source="videos/inclined_plane_1080p.mp4"
         preview="videos/previews/inclined_plane_1080p.jpg"
         width="100%" aspect="16:9"/>
</figure>

        <p>
          First, we determine the total gravitational force vector <m>\overrightarrow{F}</m> acting on the mass. 
          Using <m>g = 9.8\text{ m/s}^2</m>:
          <md>\|\overrightarrow{F}\| = (10)(9.8) = 98\text{ N}</md>
          Acting entirely in the negative vertical direction, this gives:
          <md>\overrightarrow{F} = \langle 0, -98 \rangle = -98\hat{\jmath}</md>
        </p>

        <!-- Diagram inserted here -->
        <figure xml:id="fig-inclined-forces">
          <caption>Forces acting on a mass on a <m>30^{\circ}</m> incline: the weight
          <m>\overrightarrow{F}</m> resolves into a component <m>\overrightarrow{F}_{\parallel}</m> down the
          slope and a component <m>\overrightarrow{F}_{\perp}</m> into the surface, with
          <m>\overrightarrow{F} = \overrightarrow{F}_{\parallel} + \overrightarrow{F}_{\perp}</m>.</caption>
          <image width="60%">
            <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-inclined-forces">
              <diagram dimensions="(350,300)" margins="5">
                <coordinates bbox="(-1,-2,6,4)">
                  <!-- Inclined plane: 30-degree ramp with its right-triangle base -->
                  <line at="incline-surface" p1="(0,0)" p2="(5.196,3)" stroke="black" thickness="2"/>
                  <line at="incline-base" p1="(0,0)" p2="(5.196,0)" stroke="black" thickness="1" dash="4 4"/>
                  <line at="incline-vertical" p1="(5.196,0)" p2="(5.196,3)" stroke="black" thickness="1" dash="4 4"/>
                  <label at="angle-label" anchor="(1.3,0.25)" alignment="ne"><m>30^{\circ}</m></label>

                  <!-- Block resting on the incline (rotated to match the slope) -->
                  <rectangle at="block" center="(2.398,1.846)" dimensions="(1.2,0.8)"
                             rotate="30" fill="lightgray" stroke="black"/>

                  <!-- Force vectors from the center of mass; scaled so they add to the weight -->
                  <vector at="vec-weight" v="(0,-3)" tail="(2.398,1.846)" stroke="blue" thickness="2"/>
                  <label at="weight-label" anchor="(2.75,-1.05)" alignment="e" color="blue"><m>\overrightarrow{F}</m></label>

                  <vector at="vec-parallel" v="(-1.299,-0.75)" tail="(2.398,1.846)" stroke="red" thickness="2"/>
                  <label at="parallel-label" anchor="(0.75,1.05)" alignment="w" color="red"><m>\overrightarrow{F}_{\parallel}</m></label>

                  <vector at="vec-perp" v="(1.299,-2.25)" tail="(2.398,1.846)" stroke="purple" thickness="2"/>
                  <label at="perp-label" anchor="(3.95,-0.4)" alignment="e" color="purple"><m>\overrightarrow{F}_{\perp}</m></label>
                </coordinates>

                <annotations>
                  <annotation ref="figure" text="A block resting on a thirty degree inclined plane, with the weight vector and its two components drawn from the block's center of mass.">
                    <annotation ref="incline-surface" text="The sloped surface of the incline, rising at thirty degrees from the horizontal."/>
                    <annotation ref="block" text="A rectangular block resting on the incline, tilted to lie flush with the slope."/>
                    <annotation ref="vec-weight" text="The weight vector F, pointing straight down from the center of mass."/>
                    <annotation ref="vec-parallel" text="The component of the weight parallel to the incline, pointing down the slope."/>
                    <annotation ref="vec-perp" text="The component of the weight perpendicular to the incline, pointing into the surface."/>
                  </annotation>
                </annotations>
              </diagram>
            </prefigure>
          </image>
        </figure>

        <p>
          Next, we define a unit vector <m>\hat{v}</m> pointing down the surface of the inclined plane. 
          Since the plane is tilted at <m>30^{\circ}</m> (or <m>\frac{\pi}{6}</m> radians), a unit vector pointing down the slope is directed at an angle of <m>-\frac{\pi}{6}</m> relative to the horizontal:
          <md>\hat{v} = \cos\left(\frac{\pi}{6}\right)\hat{\imath} - \sin\left(\frac{\pi}{6}\right)\hat{\jmath} = \left\langle \frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}, -\frac{1}{2} \right\rangle</md>
          where <m>\|\hat{v}\| = 1</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          The component of the force parallel to the incline, <m>\overrightarrow{F}_{\parallel}</m>, is the projection of <m>\overrightarrow{F}</m> onto <m>\hat{v}</m>, given by <xref ref="eq-proj"/>:
          <md>\overrightarrow{F}_{\parallel} = \operatorname{proj}_{\hat{v}}\overrightarrow{F} = \left(\frac{\overrightarrow{F} \cdot \hat{v}}{\|\hat{v}\|^2}\right)\hat{v} = (\overrightarrow{F} \cdot \hat{v})\hat{v}</md>
          Computing the dot product with <xref ref="eq-5"/>:
          <md>\overrightarrow{F} \cdot \hat{v} = \langle 0, -98 \rangle \cdot \left\langle \frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}, -\frac{1}{2} \right\rangle = 0 + 49 = 49</md>
          Thus, the parallel force vector is:
          <md>\overrightarrow{F}_{\parallel} = 49 \left\langle \frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}, -\frac{1}{2} \right\rangle</md>
        </p>
        <p>
          Since <m>\overrightarrow{F} = \overrightarrow{F}_{\parallel} + \overrightarrow{F}_{\perp}</m> by <xref ref="eq-decomposition"/>, the perpendicular force component is found by subtraction:
          <md>
            <mrow>\overrightarrow{F}_{\perp} \amp = \overrightarrow{F} - \overrightarrow{F}_{\parallel}</mrow>
            <mrow>\amp = \langle 0, -98 \rangle - 49 \left\langle \frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}, -\frac{1}{2} \right\rangle</mrow>
            <mrow>\amp = 49 \left( \langle 0, -2 \rangle - \left\langle \frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}, -\frac{1}{2} \right\rangle \right)</mrow>
            <mrow>\amp = 49 \left\langle -\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}, -\frac{3}{2} \right\rangle</mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
      </solution>
</example>


</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-data-science">
<title>Application in Data Science: Measuring Similarity in Tastes</title>

<p>
Every time a streaming service tells you <q>because you watched
<em>Sinners</em>&#8230;</q>, some piece of software has decided that your taste
resembles the taste of other people in its database. Deciding whose taste
resembles whose can be framed as a geometry problem, and one tool that can help
us decide is the dot product.
</p>

<p>
The first step is to turn taste into a vector. Fix a list of movies and ask
each person to rate every one of them on a scale from <m>-5</m> to <m>5</m>,
where <m>5</m> means <q>loved it</q>, <m>-5</m> means <q>I want those two hours
back</q>, and <m>0</m> means indifference. Reading the ratings off in the same
order for everybody turns each person into a vector, and the question
<q>do these two people have similar taste?</q> becomes a question about the
angle between two vectors, which by <xref ref="thm-angle-formula"/> is exactly
what the dot product measures.
</p>

<definition xml:id="def-angular-distance">
<title>Angular Distance and Cosine Similarity</title>
<statement>
<p>
Let <m>\mathbf u</m> and <m>\mathbf v</m> be two nonzero vectors. Their
<term>angular distance</term> is the angle <m>\theta</m> between them, obtained
from <xref ref="eq-4"/>,
<md xml:id="eq-angular-distance" number="yes">
d_A(\mathbf u,\mathbf v) = \theta
= \cos^{-1}\left(\frac{\mathbf u \cdot \mathbf v}{\|\mathbf u\|\|\mathbf v\|}\right).
</md>
The quantity <m>\dfrac{\mathbf u \cdot \mathbf v}{\|\mathbf u\|\|\mathbf v\|} = \cos\theta</m>
appearing inside is called the <term>cosine similarity</term> of
<m>\mathbf u</m> and <m>\mathbf v</m>. The smaller the angular distance, the
more similar the two vectors.
</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>
Angular distance asks <q>how nearly do these two vectors point the same way?</q>
Notice that it is completely blind to the lengths of <m>\mathbf u</m> and
<m>\mathbf v</m>: multiplying either vector by a positive number leaves
<m>\theta</m> untouched. We will come back to what that means for moviegoers.
</p>

<paragraphs xml:id="par-two-movies">
<title>Two Movies at a Time</title>

<p>
Two movies give vectors in <m>\mathbb R^2</m>, which we can draw. Take two of
the ten films nominated for Best Picture at the 2026 Academy Awards,
<em>Sinners</em> and <em>Hamnet</em>, and record each person's rating of
<em>Sinners</em> as the first component and of <em>Hamnet</em> as the second.
Mehdi, Norm, and Popcorn rate them as follows:
<md>
\begin{aligned}
\text{Mehdi:} \quad \mathbf u \amp = \langle 4, 5\rangle \\
\text{Norm:} \quad \mathbf v \amp = \langle 5, 3\rangle \\
\text{Popcorn:} \quad \mathbf w \amp = \langle -4, -2\rangle.
\end{aligned}
</md>
Mehdi and Norm both enjoyed the two films; Popcorn sat through both of them and
regretted it. <xref ref="fig-taste-vectors-2d"/> shows the three taste vectors.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-taste-vectors-2d">
<caption>
The taste vectors <m>\mathbf u</m> (Mehdi), <m>\mathbf v</m> (Norm), and
<m>\mathbf w</m> (Popcorn). The angle <m>\theta</m> is the angular distance
between <m>\mathbf u</m> and <m>\mathbf v</m>.
</caption>
<image width="65%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-taste-vectors-2d">
<diagram dimensions="(420,420)" margins="14">
<definition>m=(4,5)</definition>
<definition>n=(5,3)</definition>
<definition>d=(-4,-2)</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-7,-7,7,7)">
<grid-axes at="grid-taste" xlabel="\text{Sinners}" ylabel="\text{Hamnet}"/>
<vector at="vec-m" v="m" tail="(0,0)" stroke="#0072B2" thickness="2"/>
<vector at="vec-n" v="n" tail="(0,0)" stroke="#D55E00" thickness="2"/>
<vector at="vec-d" v="d" tail="(0,0)" stroke="#009E73" thickness="2"/>
<angle-marker at="angle-mn" points="(n,(0,0),m)" radius="40" clear-background="no">
<m>\theta</m>
</angle-marker>
<label at="face-m" anchor="m" alignment="nw" font-size="26">&#x1F913;</label>
<label at="face-n" anchor="n" alignment="e" font-size="26">&#x1F60E;</label>
<label at="face-d" anchor="d" alignment="sw" font-size="26">&#x1F92A;</label>
<label at="name-m" anchor="(3.2,5.5)" alignment="w">Mehdi</label>
<label at="name-n" anchor="(5.2,2.6)" alignment="se">Norm</label>
<label at="name-d" anchor="(-4.2,-2.9)" alignment="s">Popcorn</label>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="A coordinate grid whose horizontal axis is the rating of the film Sinners and whose vertical axis is the rating of the film Hamnet, both running from negative 5 to 5. Three vectors are drawn from the origin: a blue vector to the point 4 comma 5, labeled Mehdi; an orange vector to the point 5 comma 3, labeled Norm; and a green vector to the point negative 4 comma negative 2, labeled Popcorn. The blue and orange vectors point in nearly the same direction and a small angle theta is marked between them, while the green vector points in almost the opposite direction.">
  <annotation ref="grid-taste" text="A coordinate grid with the Sinners rating on the horizontal axis and the Hamnet rating on the vertical axis."/>
  <annotation ref="vec-m" text="Mehdi's taste vector u equals 4 comma 5, drawn in blue from the origin."/>
  <annotation ref="vec-n" text="Norm's taste vector v equals 5 comma 3, drawn in orange from the origin."/>
  <annotation ref="vec-d" text="Popcorn's taste vector w equals negative 4 comma negative 2, drawn in green from the origin, pointing into the third quadrant."/>
  <annotation ref="angle-mn" text="The angle theta between u and v, measured at the origin, is about 20 degrees."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>

<example xml:id="example-taste-2d">
<title>Who should watch a movie together?</title>
<statement>
<p>
Using the taste vectors above, rank the three pairs by angular distance.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
The three dot products are
<md>
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf u \cdot \mathbf v \amp = 20 + 15 = 35, \\
\mathbf u \cdot \mathbf w \amp = -16 - 10 = -26, \\
\mathbf v \cdot \mathbf w \amp = -20 - 6 = -26,
\end{aligned}
</md>
and the lengths are <m>\|\mathbf u\| = \sqrt{41}</m>,
<m>\|\mathbf v\| = \sqrt{34}</m>, and <m>\|\mathbf w\| = \sqrt{20}</m>. Then, by
<xref ref="eq-angular-distance"/>,
<md>
\begin{aligned}
\text{Mehdi, Norm:} \quad \cos\theta \amp = \frac{35}{\sqrt{41}\,\sqrt{34}} \approx 0.9374 \\[2pt]
\text{Mehdi, Popcorn:} \quad \cos\theta \amp = \frac{-26}{\sqrt{41}\,\sqrt{20}} \approx -0.9080 \\[2pt]
\text{Norm, Popcorn:} \quad \cos\theta \amp = \frac{-26}{\sqrt{34}\,\sqrt{20}} \approx -0.9971.
\end{aligned}
</md>
Taking <m>\cos^{-1}</m> of each of these gives the three angular distances.
</p>
<table xml:id="table-taste-2d">
<title>Angular distance between the three taste vectors.</title>
<tabular halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
<row header="yes" bottom="major">
<cell>Pair</cell>
<cell><m>\cos\theta</m></cell>
<cell><m>d_A</m></cell>
</row>
<row><cell>Mehdi, Norm</cell><cell><m>0.9374</m></cell><cell><m>20.4^\circ</m></cell></row>
<row><cell>Mehdi, Popcorn</cell><cell><m>-0.9080</m></cell><cell><m>155.2^\circ</m></cell></row>
<row><cell>Norm, Popcorn</cell><cell><m>-0.9971</m></cell><cell><m>175.6^\circ</m></cell></row>
</tabular>
</table>
<p>
Mehdi and Norm are the movie buddies. The sign of the dot product already told
us most of the story: <m>\mathbf u \cdot \mathbf v &gt; 0</m> means an acute
angle and broadly shared taste, while <m>\mathbf v \cdot \mathbf w &lt; 0</m>
means an obtuse angle, and here an angle of nearly <m>180^\circ</m> &#8212; Norm
and Popcorn are as close to being opposites as two moviegoers can be.
</p>
</solution>
</example>
</paragraphs>

<paragraphs xml:id="par-scale-invariance">
<title>Angular Distance Ignores Enthusiasm</title>

<p>
Suppose Ray, Vera, and Gus rate the same two films
<md>
\begin{aligned}
\text{Ray:} \quad \mathbf a \amp = \langle 2, 2\rangle \\
\text{Vera:} \quad \mathbf b \amp = \langle 5, 4\rangle \\
\text{Gus:} \quad \mathbf c \amp = \langle 5, 5\rangle,
\end{aligned}
</md>
as in <xref ref="fig-taste-disagree"/>. Since <m>\mathbf c = \tfrac52\mathbf a</m>,
the vectors <m>\mathbf a</m> and <m>\mathbf c</m> point in exactly the same
direction, so <m>d_A(\mathbf a,\mathbf c) = 0^\circ</m>: as far as angular
distance is concerned, Ray and Gus have identical taste. Vera sits about
<m>6.3^\circ</m> away from that shared direction, since
<md>
\cos\theta = \frac{\mathbf b \cdot \mathbf c}{\|\mathbf b\|\|\mathbf c\|}
= \frac{25+20}{\sqrt{41}\,\sqrt{50}} \approx 0.9939.
</md>
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-taste-disagree">
<caption>
Ray, Vera, and Gus. Ray's vector <m>\mathbf a</m> lies along Gus's vector
<m>\mathbf c</m>, so their angular distance is <m>0</m>, even though Gus handed
out far more generous ratings.
</caption>
<image width="60%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-taste-disagree">
<diagram dimensions="(420,330)" margins="14">
<definition>r=(2,2)</definition>
<definition>v=(5,4)</definition>
<definition>g=(5,5)</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-0.8,-0.8,7.4,6.4)">
<grid-axes at="grid-disagree" xlabel="\text{Sinners}" ylabel="\text{Hamnet}"/>
<vector at="vec-g" v="g" tail="(0,0)" stroke="#009E73" thickness="2"/>
<vector at="vec-v" v="v" tail="(0,0)" stroke="#D55E00" thickness="2"/>
<vector at="vec-r" v="r" tail="(0,0)" stroke="#0072B2" thickness="3"/>
<label at="face-r" anchor="(2.2,1.9)" alignment="se" font-size="24">&#x1F610;</label>
<label at="face-v" anchor="v" alignment="se" font-size="24">&#x1F929;</label>
<label at="face-g" anchor="g" alignment="ne" font-size="24">&#x1F973;</label>
<label at="name-r" anchor="(2.85,1.5)" alignment="e">Ray</label>
<label at="name-v" anchor="(5.6,3.4)" alignment="e">Vera</label>
<label at="name-g" anchor="(5.6,5.3)" alignment="e">Gus</label>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="A coordinate grid whose horizontal axis is the rating of Sinners and whose vertical axis is the rating of Hamnet, both running from 0 to about 6. A short thick blue vector runs from the origin to the point 2 comma 2, labeled Ray. A longer green vector runs from the origin to the point 5 comma 5, labeled Gus, and lies along exactly the same line as the blue vector. An orange vector runs from the origin to the point 5 comma 4, labeled Vera, just below the green one.">
  <annotation ref="grid-disagree" text="A coordinate grid with the Sinners rating on the horizontal axis and the Hamnet rating on the vertical axis."/>
  <annotation ref="vec-r" text="Ray's taste vector a equals 2 comma 2, drawn as a short thick blue vector from the origin."/>
  <annotation ref="vec-g" text="Gus's taste vector c equals 5 comma 5, drawn in green from the origin along the same line as Ray's vector."/>
  <annotation ref="vec-v" text="Vera's taste vector b equals 5 comma 4, drawn in orange from the origin, slightly below Gus's vector."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
This is the scale invariance noted after
<xref ref="def-angular-distance"/>, seen from the moviegoers' side. Ray is a
cautious rater and Gus is an enthusiastic one, but they ranked the two films
the same way and in the same proportion, and angular distance sees only that
shared direction. If you regard a lukewarm reviewer and a euphoric one who
agree about every film as having the same taste, this is exactly the behavior
you want.
</p>
</paragraphs>

<paragraphs xml:id="par-more-movies">
<title>More Movies, More Dimensions</title>

<p>
Nothing above depended on there being exactly two movies. With <m>n</m> movies,
a person's taste vector is
<m>\mathbf u = \langle u_1, u_2, \ldots, u_n\rangle</m> in <m>\mathbb R^n</m>,
the dot product of <xref ref="eq-5"/> becomes a sum of <m>n</m> products,
<m>\mathbf u \cdot \mathbf v = \sum_{i=1}^{n} u_i v_i</m>, and the length of a
vector becomes <m>\|\mathbf u\| = \sqrt{\sum_{i=1}^{n} u_i^2}</m>. The angular
distance of <xref ref="def-angular-distance"/> carries over verbatim:
<md xml:id="eq-similarity-n" number="yes">
d_A(\mathbf u,\mathbf v) = \cos^{-1}\left(
\frac{\sum_{i=1}^{n} u_i v_i}
{\sqrt{\sum_{i=1}^{n} u_i^2}\,\sqrt{\sum_{i=1}^{n} v_i^2}}\right).
</md>
</p>

<p>
We can no longer draw the picture, but every formula still applies, and the
language of <xref ref="remark-orthogonal"/> still means something: two taste
vectors with <m>\mathbf u \cdot \mathbf v = 0</m> are orthogonal, an angular
distance of <m>90^\circ</m>, which here says that knowing one person's ratings
tells you nothing about the other's. This is what makes the dot product useful
in practice. A streaming service with <m>50{,}000</m> titles works in
<m>\mathbb R^{50{,}000}</m>, and finding the users nearest to you is still
nothing more than the arithmetic in <xref ref="eq-similarity-n"/>.
</p>

<example xml:id="example-taste-10d">
<title>All ten nominees</title>
<statement>
<p>
List the ten 2026 Best Picture nominees alphabetically&#8212;<em>Bugonia</em>,
<em>F1</em>, <em>Frankenstein</em>, <em>Hamnet</em>, <em>Marty Supreme</em>,
<em>One Battle After Another</em>, <em>The Secret Agent</em>,
<em>Sentimental Value</em>, <em>Sinners</em>, <em>Train Dreams</em>&#8212;and
suppose Mehdi, Norm, and Popcorn rate all ten:
<md>
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf u \amp = \langle 3,\,-1,\,2,\,5,\,0,\,5,\,4,\,3,\,4,\,2\rangle \\
\mathbf v \amp = \langle 1,\,4,\,3,\,3,\,5,\,4,\,0,\,1,\,5,\,-2\rangle \\
\mathbf w \amp = \langle -2,\,3,\,-3,\,-2,\,4,\,-1,\,-4,\,-5,\,-4,\,-3\rangle.
\end{aligned}
</md>
(The fourth and ninth entries are the <em>Hamnet</em> and <em>Sinners</em>
ratings from before.) Which pair has the most similar taste?
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
The dot products are <m>\mathbf u \cdot \mathbf v = 59</m>,
<m>\mathbf u \cdot \mathbf w = -83</m>, and
<m>\mathbf v \cdot \mathbf w = -8</m>, and the lengths are
<m>\|\mathbf u\| = \sqrt{109}</m>, <m>\|\mathbf v\| = \sqrt{106}</m>, and
<m>\|\mathbf w\| = \sqrt{109}</m>. Applying
<xref ref="eq-similarity-n"/> to each pair gives the following.
</p>
<table xml:id="table-taste-10d">
<title>Angular distance across all ten nominees.</title>
<tabular halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
<row header="yes" bottom="major">
<cell>Pair</cell>
<cell><m>\cos\theta</m></cell>
<cell><m>d_A</m></cell>
</row>
<row><cell>Mehdi, Norm</cell><cell><m>0.549</m></cell><cell><m>56.7^\circ</m></cell></row>
<row><cell>Mehdi, Popcorn</cell><cell><m>-0.762</m></cell><cell><m>139.6^\circ</m></cell></row>
<row><cell>Norm, Popcorn</cell><cell><m>-0.074</m></cell><cell><m>94.3^\circ</m></cell></row>
</tabular>
</table>
<p>
Mehdi and Norm are again the closest pair, though the extra eight films have
pulled them apart: their angular distance grew from <m>20.4^\circ</m> to
<m>56.7^\circ</m> once we stopped judging them on <em>Sinners</em> and
<em>Hamnet</em> alone. Norm and Popcorn are nearly orthogonal, with an angle of
<m>94.3^\circ</m>; their tastes are essentially unrelated rather than opposed,
which is precisely the distinction the two-movie picture was too small to make.
</p>
</solution>
</example>

<p>
Ten components are a lot to handle by hand, and a real recommender system deals
with thousands. The cell below carries out the computation of
<xref ref="example-taste-10d"/>. Press <em>Evaluate</em> to run it, then edit
the ratings and run it again to see whose taste your own vector is closest to.
</p>

<sage>
<input>
# ratings of the ten nominees, in alphabetical order
u = vector([ 3, -1,  2,  5,  0,  5,  4,  3,  4,  2])
v = vector([ 1,  4,  3,  3,  5,  4,  0,  1,  5, -2])
w = vector([-2,  3, -3, -2,  4, -1, -4, -5, -4, -3])

def cosine_similarity(a, b):
    return a.dot_product(b) / (a.norm() * b.norm())

# the angular distance, converted from radians to degrees
def angular_distance(a, b):
    return arccos(cosine_similarity(a, b)) * 180 / pi

for name, a, b in [("Mehdi, Norm", u, v),
                   ("Mehdi, Popcorn", u, w),
                   ("Norm, Popcorn", v, w)]:
    print(name, N(cosine_similarity(a, b), digits=3), N(angular_distance(a, b), digits=4))
</input>
<output>
Mehdi, Norm 0.549 56.71
Mehdi, Popcorn -0.762 139.6
Norm, Popcorn -0.0744 94.27
</output>
</sage>
</paragraphs>

</subsection>

</section>